\lessonlabel{1}
\section{Introduction to Management}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define the concept of management;
  \item list the four basic functions of management;
  \item describe the three levels of management in an organisation;
  \item identify key skills required of effective managers.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is management?}

\term{Management} is the process of planning, organising, leading and controlling resources to achieve organisational goals efficiently and effectively. It involves making decisions, allocating resources, and guiding people toward desired outcomes.

\begin{example}
A small café owner decides the menu (planning), orders supplies and schedules staff (organising), motivates employees during a busy lunch (leading), and checks daily sales to adjust prices if needed (controlling). This whole sequence is management.
\end{example}

\subsection{The four functions of management}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Planning} – setting objectives and deciding how to achieve them.
  \item \term{Organising} – arranging tasks, people and resources to implement the plan.
  \item \term{Leading} – influencing and motivating people to work toward the goals.
  \item \term{Controlling} – monitoring performance and making corrections as needed.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A university department plans to increase enrolment (planning), creates new course sections and hires adjunct lecturers (organising), holds faculty meetings to communicate the vision (leading), and reviews enrolment numbers each semester to adjust marketing efforts (controlling).
\end{example}

\subsection{Levels of management}

Organisations typically have three hierarchical levels:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Top management} – sets overall direction, formulates strategy, and represents the organisation to external stakeholders.
  \item \term{Middle management} – translates strategic goals into operational plans, coordinates departments, and reports to top management.
  \item \term{First‑line (operational) management} – supervises daily activities, manages frontline staff, and ensures tasks are completed on time.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
In a retail chain, the CEO (top management) decides to expand into a new market, the regional manager (middle management) develops a rollout plan for stores, and the store manager (first‑line) schedules staff shifts and monitors sales each day.
\end{example}

\subsection{Key managerial skills}

Effective managers need a mix of skills:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Technical skills} – knowledge of specific tools, processes or industry practices.
  \item \term{Human skills} – ability to communicate, motivate and work with people.
  \item \term{Conceptual skills} – capacity to see the whole organisation, understand relationships and think strategically.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A project manager uses technical skills to understand software development tools, human skills to resolve team conflicts, and conceptual skills to align the project with the company’s long‑term strategy.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Ask yourself: Do I need to set goals, organise resources, guide people and check results? If yes, I am performing management. Identify which of the four functions I am using most in a given situation.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write a short paragraph defining management in your own words.
  \item For a student club, list one activity that belongs to each of the four management functions.
  \item Match the following roles to the appropriate level of management: (a) Chief Financial Officer, (b) Department Supervisor, (c) Team Leader.
  \item Give an example of a situation where a manager needs strong conceptual skills rather than technical skills.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Management & Planning, organising, leading and controlling resources to achieve goals. \\
Planning & Setting objectives and deciding how to achieve them. \\
Organising & Arranging tasks, people and resources to implement plans. \\
Leading & Influencing and motivating people toward goals. \\
Controlling & Monitoring performance and correcting deviations. \\
Top management & Senior executives who set overall direction and strategy. \\
Middle management & Managers who translate strategy into operational plans. \\
First‑line management & Supervisors who oversee daily activities and staff. \\
Technical skills & Knowledge of specific tools or processes. \\
Human skills & Ability to communicate and work with people. \\
Conceptual skills & Ability to understand the organisation as a whole and think strategically. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Evolution of Management Thought}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item list the major schools of management thought;
  \item describe the key ideas of each school;
  \item explain how the schools relate to changes in industry and society;
  \item identify which school best fits a given organisational problem.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Classical management school}

The classical school emerged in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. It focuses on efficiency, formal structure and scientific analysis of work.

\term{Scientific management} (Frederick Taylor) proposes that work should be studied scientifically, broken into small tasks, and workers trained to perform each task in the “one best way”.  

\term{Administrative management} (Henri Fayol) identifies five functions of management – planning, organising, commanding, coordinating and controlling – and fourteen principles such as unity of command and scalar chain.

\begin{example}
A factory measures the time to assemble a widget, determines that 5 minutes is the optimal time, and sets a standard work sheet for all operators. This standardisation is a direct application of scientific management.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Does the problem involve improving the speed or cost of a repetitive task? If yes, the classical approach is likely appropriate.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Human relations school}

The human relations school arose in the 1930s as a reaction to the mechanistic view of the classical school. It stresses the importance of social factors, employee morale and group dynamics.

\term{Hawthorne studies} (Elton Mayo) showed that workers’ productivity increased when they felt observed and valued, leading to the concept of “informal organisation”.

\term{Motivation theories} such as Maslow’s hierarchy of needs and Herzberg’s two‑factor theory argue that employees have psychological needs that must be satisfied for high performance.

\begin{example}
A manager holds regular informal meetings where staff can voice concerns and suggest improvements. Attendance and job satisfaction rise, illustrating the human relations emphasis on communication and participation.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Is the main issue low employee morale, poor communication, or high turnover? If so, apply human relations ideas.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Systems approach}

The systems approach developed in the 1950s and 1960s, viewing an organisation as an open system that interacts with its environment.

\term{Open system} means that inputs (resources, information) are transformed into outputs (products, services) and feedback from the environment influences future actions.

\term{Subsystems} such as production, finance and marketing are interdependent; a change in one affects the others.

\begin{example}
A company adopts a new inventory‑management software. The change improves production scheduling, reduces costs in finance, and alters the marketing promise of faster delivery, showing the interdependence of subsystems.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Does the problem involve multiple departments or external factors such as market changes? If yes, the systems perspective is useful.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Contingency approach}

The contingency approach, popular from the 1960s onward, argues that there is no single best way to manage. The appropriate style depends on situational variables.

\term{Contingency variables} include organisational size, technology, environment stability and workforce skills.

\term{Fit} means aligning structure, leadership style and processes with these variables.

\begin{example}
A start‑up with a small, highly skilled team uses a flat, informal structure, while a large, mature manufacturing firm adopts a hierarchical bureaucracy. Both choices fit their respective contingencies.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify the key variables of the situation. Choose the management style that best matches those variables.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Match each management school with its principal founder(s).
  \item Explain how the Hawthorne studies changed the view of worker motivation.
  \item A company faces rapid market change and internal conflict between sales and production. Which approach would help most and why?
  \item Describe a real‑world example where a contingency fit improved performance.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Scientific management & Study of work to find the most efficient method. \\
Administrative management & Five functions and fourteen principles of management. \\
Human relations & Focus on employee needs, morale and informal groups. \\
Open system & Organisation that exchanges inputs and outputs with its environment. \\
Subsystem & A distinct part of an organisation that interacts with others. \\
Contingency approach & Management style chosen based on situational factors. \\
Fit & Alignment of structure and processes with environmental variables. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{3}
\section{Planning and Decision‑Making}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define what planning means in a project context;
  \item list the main steps of a planning process;
  \item describe common decision‑making models;
  \item apply a simple decision‑making technique to a practical problem.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is planning?}

\term{Planning} is the activity of setting goals, deciding how to achieve them, and arranging resources and time. A plan shows what must be done, who will do it, and when it should be finished. For example, a student group planning a workshop writes down the topics, assigns speakers, books a room, and creates a timetable.

\begin{example}
A university club wants to host a guest lecture. The planning steps are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Choose a date and venue.
  \item Invite the speaker and confirm availability.
  \item Advertise the event to students.
  \item Arrange equipment and refreshments.
  \item Assign volunteers for registration and technical support.
\end{enumerate}
\end{example}

\subsection{Steps in the planning process}

A typical planning process contains four stages:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Goal definition}. State clearly what the project should achieve.
  \item \term{Task identification}. Break the goal into smaller, manageable tasks.
  \item \term{Scheduling}. Order the tasks, estimate durations, and allocate time slots.
  \item \term{Resource allocation}. Assign people, budget, and equipment to each task.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A student project aims to develop a simple mobile app. The goal is “deliver a working prototype in eight weeks”. Tasks include “design UI”, “write code”, “test features”, and “prepare presentation”. Each task is given a two‑week slot and a team member is assigned.
\end{example}

\subsection{Decision‑making models}

Decision‑making chooses the best alternative among several options. Two common models are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Rational model}. List all alternatives, evaluate them against criteria, and select the highest‑scoring option.
  \item \term{Bounded rationality}. Recognise that information and time are limited; choose a satisfactory option rather than the optimal one.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A student must pick a topic for a term paper. Using the rational model, she lists three topics, scores each on interest (1‑5), available sources (1‑5), and workload (1‑5). The topic with the highest total score is chosen.
\end{example}

\subsection{Simple decision‑making technique: weighted scoring}

Weighted scoring combines criteria and their importance:

\[
\text{Score}(A)=\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i \times r_{i}(A)
\]

where \(w_i\) is the weight of criterion \(i\) (sum of weights = 1) and \(r_{i}(A)\) is the rating of alternative \(A\) on that criterion (usually 0–5).

\begin{example}
Choosing a study location. Criteria: quietness (0.4), distance from home (0.3), Wi‑Fi speed (0.3). Ratings for Library: 5, 2, 4. Score = 0.4·5 + 0.3·2 + 0.3·4 = 2.0 + 0.6 + 1.2 = 3.8. Ratings for Café: 3, 5, 5. Score = 0.4·3 + 0.3·5 + 0.3·5 = 1.2 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 4.2. The café scores higher, so it is the preferred choice.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} When planning a new activity, list the goal, at least three tasks, and assign a person to each task. Then, for a decision you must make, write down two criteria, give them weights that add to 1, rate two alternatives, and compute the weighted scores.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Write a short goal statement for a group project you are involved in.
  \item Identify five tasks needed to achieve that goal and suggest a realistic order.
  \item Using the weighted scoring method, decide between two software tools for your project. Choose two criteria, assign weights, rate each tool, and compute the scores.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Planning & Setting goals, deciding actions, and arranging resources and time. \\
Goal definition & Stating clearly what the project intends to achieve. \\
Task identification & Breaking a goal into smaller, manageable activities. \\
Scheduling & Ordering tasks and assigning time periods. \\
Resource allocation & Assigning people, money, and equipment to tasks. \\
Decision‑making & Choosing the best alternative among options. \\
Rational model & Systematic evaluation of all alternatives against criteria. \\
Bounded rationality & Choosing a satisfactory option when information or time is limited. \\
Weighted scoring & A technique that multiplies ratings by criterion weights and sums them. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{4}
\section{Organizing and Structure}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item describe the purpose of logical organization in a database;
  \item differentiate between tables, records and fields;
  \item explain primary keys, foreign keys and their role in structure;
  \item apply basic normalization to avoid redundancy.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Logical organization}

A \term{database schema} is the logical blueprint of how data is arranged. It tells us what tables exist, what columns each table has, and how tables relate. The schema is independent of the physical files that store the data.

\begin{example}
In a university system the schema may contain a \texttt{Students} table (fields: StudentID, Name, Email) and a \texttt{Enrollments} table (fields: EnrollmentID, StudentID, CourseID, Grade). The schema shows that \texttt{StudentID} links the two tables.
\end{example}

\subsection{Tables, records and fields}

\term{Table} is a collection of rows that share the same set of columns. Each row is a \term{record}, and each column is a \term{field}. Fields hold single pieces of data, such as a name or a date.

\begin{example}
A record in the \texttt{Students} table might be (1023, “Ada Lovelace”, “ada@example.com”). Here 1023 is the value of the \texttt{StudentID} field, “Ada Lovelace” the \texttt{Name} field, and so on.
\end{example}

\subsection{Primary keys}

A \term{primary key} uniquely identifies each record in a table. No two records can have the same primary‑key value, and the key cannot be null. It provides a stable reference for other tables.

\begin{example}
In the \texttt{Students} table the field \texttt{StudentID} is declared as the primary key. Every student gets a different number, so the DBMS can always locate a specific student.
\end{example}

\subsection{Foreign keys and relationships}

A \term{foreign key} is a field (or set of fields) in one table that refers to the primary key of another table. This creates a \term{relationship} between the tables and enforces referential integrity.

\begin{example}
The \texttt{Enrollments} table contains a \texttt{StudentID} field that is a foreign key referencing \texttt{Students.StudentID}. The DBMS will reject an enrollment record if the referenced student does not exist.
\end{example}

\subsection{Normalization basics}

Normalization is the process of organising tables to reduce redundancy and improve integrity. The first three normal forms are most common:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{First Normal Form (1NF).} Each field contains only atomic (indivisible) values; no repeating groups.
  \item \textbf{Second Normal Form (2NF).} The table is in 1NF and every non‑key field depends on the whole primary key.
  \item \textbf{Third Normal Form (3NF).} The table is in 2NF and no non‑key field depends on another non‑key field.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A table with columns \texttt{StudentID, StudentName, CourseID, CourseName} violates 3NF because \texttt{CourseName} depends on \texttt{CourseID}, not on the primary key (\texttt{StudentID, CourseID}). Splitting into \texttt{Students} and \texttt{Courses} tables removes the redundancy.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Check a table: (1) Are all values atomic? (2) Does every non‑key field depend on the whole primary key? (3) Is any non‑key field dependent on another non‑key field? If the answer is yes to any, the table needs further normalisation.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the primary key in a table with columns \texttt{ISBN, Title, Author, Publisher}.
  \item Given a table \texttt{Orders(OrderID, CustomerID, CustomerName, OrderDate)}, explain why \texttt{CustomerName} should be moved to a separate \texttt{Customers} table.
  \item Write the foreign‑key definition that links a \texttt{LineItems} table (field \texttt{OrderID}) to the \texttt{Orders} table.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Schema & Logical description of tables, fields and relationships. \\
Table & Set of records sharing the same fields. \\
Record & Single row in a table, representing one entity. \\
Field & Column in a table, holding a specific attribute. \\
Primary key & Unique, non‑null identifier for records in a table. \\
Foreign key & Field that references a primary key in another table. \\
Normalization & Process of structuring tables to reduce redundancy. \\
1NF & Each field contains only atomic values. \\
2NF & Non‑key fields depend on the whole primary key. \\
3NF & No non‑key field depends on another non‑key field. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{5}
\section{Leadership Styles and Motivation}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item identify the main \term{leadership styles};
  \item explain how each style influences \term{motivation} of team members;
  \item apply a simple model to choose an appropriate style for a given situation.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Leadership styles}

\term{Autocratic leadership} is a style in which the leader makes decisions alone and expects compliance. It can be effective when quick decisions are needed, but it may reduce intrinsic motivation because members have little input.

\term{Democratic leadership} involves the leader sharing decision‑making with the group. This often increases motivation by giving members a sense of ownership, though it can be slower.

\term{Laissez‑faire leadership} gives members high autonomy and minimal direction. Motivation may rise for self‑driven individuals, but can fall if guidance is lacking.

\begin{example}
A project team must choose a software platform. The manager (autocratic) decides instantly to use Platform X, saving time but causing some developers to feel their expertise is ignored. In a democratic meeting, the same team discusses options, votes, and selects Platform Y, leading to higher enthusiasm even though the meeting takes longer.
\end{example}

\subsection{Motivation concepts}

\term{Intrinsic motivation} is the internal drive to do something because it is interesting or satisfying. Leaders who provide meaningful tasks and autonomy tend to boost this type of motivation.

\term{Extrinsic motivation} relies on external rewards such as pay, bonuses, or recognition. An autocratic leader may use extrinsic rewards to maintain performance.

\term{Maslow's hierarchy of needs} suggests that people are motivated by a progression from basic physiological needs to self‑actualisation. Leadership style can address different levels: safety needs may be met by clear rules (autocratic), while self‑actualisation is fostered by empowerment (democratic).

\begin{example}
A sales team receives a monthly bonus (extrinsic) for meeting targets. The manager also encourages team members to set personal development goals, offering training sessions (intrinsic). The combination raises overall performance.
\end{example}

\subsection{Choosing a style for motivation}

A simple decision matrix can help match style to context:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Situation} & \text{Preferred style} & \text{Motivation focus} \\
\hline
Crisis, urgent deadline & \text{Autocratic} & \text{Extrinsic (clear rewards/punishments)} \\
Creative project, high expertise & \text{Democratic} & \text{Intrinsic (autonomy, mastery)} \\
Highly skilled, self‑directed work & \text{Laissez‑faire} & \text{Intrinsic (self‑actualisation)} \\
\end{array}
\]

\begin{example}
A software startup is developing a prototype in two weeks. The founder adopts an autocratic style, assigning tasks and setting strict milestones to meet the deadline. Once the prototype is delivered, the team shifts to a democratic style for feature planning, encouraging each member to propose ideas.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For a given team scenario, identify the dominant need (e.g., speed, creativity, autonomy) and select the leadership style that best aligns with that need to maximise motivation.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Match each leadership style (autocratic, democratic, laissez‑faire) with the type of motivation it most directly supports.
  \item A hospital unit faces a sudden influx of patients. Which style is most appropriate and why?
  \item Using the decision matrix, decide the style for a research group that must publish a paper in three months while also encouraging innovative methods.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Leadership style & The pattern of behaviour a leader uses to influence a group. \\
Autocratic & Leader makes decisions alone; low member input. \\
Democratic & Leader shares decision‑making; high member input. \\
Laissez‑faire & Leader provides minimal direction; high autonomy. \\
Motivation & The forces that drive individuals to act. \\
Intrinsic motivation & Motivation arising from internal satisfaction. \\
Extrinsic motivation & Motivation driven by external rewards or penalties. \\
Maslow's hierarchy & A theory that needs progress from basic to self‑actualisation. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{6}
\section{Communication in Organizations}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{communication} and its purpose in organisations;
  \item distinguish the main channels of organisational communication;
  \item describe the roles of formal and informal communication;
  \item identify common barriers to effective communication and ways to overcome them.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Definition and purpose}

\term{Communication} is the process of transmitting information, ideas, or feelings from a sender to a receiver. In an organisation, communication coordinates work, builds relationships, and supports decision‑making. For example, a manager sends an email to the team outlining a new project deadline; the email conveys the required information and creates a shared understanding of the task.

\subsection{Channels of organisational communication}

Organisations use several \term{channels} to move messages:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Written channels} – emails, reports, memos, policies.
  \item \textbf{Oral channels} – meetings, telephone calls, video conferences.
  \item \textbf{Electronic channels} – instant messaging, intranet, social media platforms.
  \item \textbf{Physical channels} – notice boards, flyers, face‑to‑face conversations.
\end{itemize}

Each channel has strengths. Written messages provide a record; oral channels allow immediate feedback; electronic channels enable rapid distribution across locations.

\begin{example}
A sales department uses a shared spreadsheet (electronic) to record daily sales, sends a weekly summary report (written) to senior management, and holds a short video call (oral) each Monday to discuss any issues.
\end{example}

\subsection{Formal vs. informal communication}

\term{Formal communication} follows official structures and documented procedures. Examples include policy manuals, official memos, and scheduled meetings. It ensures consistency and accountability.

\term{Informal communication} occurs spontaneously, such as casual chats, water‑cooler talk, or private messages. It can spread information quickly, build trust, and reveal employee concerns that formal channels miss.

Both types are necessary: formal channels provide clarity and authority, while informal channels foster a supportive culture.

\begin{example}
An employee learns about a new software rollout from the official announcement (formal) but also hears from a colleague that the training schedule will be adjusted (informal). Together, the two sources give a complete picture.
\end{example}

\subsection{Barriers to effective communication}

Common obstacles include:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Noise} – physical (e.g., loud environment) or psychological (e.g., pre‑conceptions) distractions.
  \item \term{Language differences} – jargon, technical terms, or cultural language gaps.
  \item \term{Information overload} – too many messages dilute important content.
  \item \term{Hierarchical barriers} – fear of speaking up to superiors.
  \item \term{Technological issues} – unreliable platforms or lack of access.
\end{enumerate}

Ways to overcome them: simplify language, use appropriate channels, encourage feedback, provide training, and establish clear communication protocols.

\begin{example}
A project manager notices that team members miss deadline reminders. She switches from a long email (information overload) to a concise instant‑message alert and adds a brief verbal reminder in the daily stand‑up, reducing noise and improving receipt.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Choose a recent organisational message you received. Identify the channel, whether it was formal or informal, and any barrier that might have affected its clarity. Propose one change to improve the communication.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define communication in your own words and give a workplace example.
  \item List three written and three oral channels used in a typical university department.
  \item Explain how informal communication can complement formal policies.
  \item Identify a barrier you have experienced and suggest a concrete step to reduce it.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Communication & Transmission of information, ideas, or feelings between sender and receiver. \\
Channel & The medium through which a message is sent (written, oral, electronic, physical). \\
Formal communication & Official, structured messages following organisational procedures. \\
Informal communication & Spontaneous, unofficial exchanges that occur naturally among members. \\
Noise & Any distraction that interferes with the accurate receipt of a message. \\
Language differences & Variations in terminology, jargon, or cultural expressions that hinder understanding. \\
Information overload & Excessive amount of information that reduces the ability to process key messages. \\
Hierarchical barrier & Reluctance to communicate across levels of authority. \\
Technological issue & Problems with the tools or platforms used for communication. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{7}
\section{Managing Teams and Groups}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define a team and a group in an organisational context;
  \item describe the stages of team development;
  \item explain at least three techniques for effective team leadership;
  \item identify common problems in team dynamics and suggest a remedy;
  \item apply a simple tool for allocating tasks within a team.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Team versus group}

A \term{team} is a small collection of people who share a common goal, have interdependent tasks, and are jointly accountable for the outcome. A \term{group} is a larger set of individuals who may share a broad interest or affiliation but work largely independently.

\begin{example}
In a software project, the developers, tester and designer form a team because they must coordinate to deliver the product. All employees in the IT department form a group; they belong to the same department but each works on separate tasks.
\end{example}

\subsection{Stages of team development}

Bruce Tuckman's model describes four stages:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Forming} – members meet, learn the purpose, and are polite.
  \item \term{Storming} – conflicts appear as people assert ideas and roles.
  \item \term{Norming} – norms develop, cooperation improves, and trust builds.
  \item \term{Performing} – the team works efficiently toward the goal.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A new student project team first introduces themselves (forming), then argues over the best design approach (storming), later agrees on a shared schedule (norming), and finally completes the prototype on time (performing).
\end{example}

\subsection{Techniques for effective team leadership}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Clear goal setting} – state specific, measurable objectives.
  \item \term{Role clarification} – assign each member a defined responsibility.
  \item \term{Regular feedback} – hold short debriefs after each task.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
The team leader writes: “We will finish the user interface by Friday (goal). Alice will design the layout, Bob will code the front‑end, and Carol will test each screen (roles). After each day we will meet for 10 minutes to discuss progress (feedback).”
\end{example}

\subsection{Common problems and remedies}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Social loafing} – some members contribute less. Remedy: make individual contributions visible, e.g., a task board.
  \item \term{Communication breakdown} – messages are missed. Remedy: use a single channel for updates and confirm receipt.
  \item \term{Role ambiguity} – unclear responsibilities. Remedy: write a brief role description for each member.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
When the team noticed that the testing deadline was missed, the leader introduced a Kanban board showing who owned each card. Visibility reduced the unnoticed delays.
\end{example}

\subsection{Simple task allocation tool: RACI matrix}

RACI stands for \term{Responsible}, \term{Accountable}, \term{Consulted}, \term{Informed}. For each task, assign one person as Responsible (does the work) and one as Accountable (owns the result). Others may be consulted or merely informed.

\begin{example}
Task: Prepare project report.
\begin{tabular}{l l}
Responsible: Bob \\
Accountable: Alice \\
Consulted: Carol (data analyst) \\
Informed: All team members
\end{tabular}
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} For a given activity list, create a RACI matrix. Verify that each task has exactly one Responsible and one Accountable person, and that no person is overloaded with more than two Responsible tasks.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Distinguish between a team and a group by giving a concrete example from university life.
  \item Sketch the Tuckman stages for a group you have worked with, noting a specific event that marked each stage.
  \item Using the RACI template, allocate roles for three tasks in a hypothetical group project.
  \item Identify a situation where social loafing occurred and propose a concrete step to prevent it in the future.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Team & Small group with shared goal, interdependent tasks, joint accountability. \\
Group & Larger collection of individuals with a common affiliation but independent work. \\
Forming & Initial stage where members meet and learn the purpose. \\
Storming & Stage of conflict as roles and ideas are contested. \\
Norming & Stage where norms and trust develop. \\
Performing & Stage of efficient, goal‑focused work. \\
RACI & Matrix assigning Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed roles for tasks. \\
Social loafing & Tendency of some members to reduce effort when working in a group. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{8}
\section{Controlling and Performance Measurement}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{controlling} in a management context;
  \item describe the steps of the controlling process;
  \item explain common performance measurement tools such as budgets, variance analysis and balanced scorecards;
  \item apply a simple variance calculation to a given set of data.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{What is controlling?}

\term{Controlling} is the management function that monitors actual performance, compares it with planned targets, and takes corrective action when needed. It closes the loop between planning and execution.

\begin{example}
A manufacturing firm plans to produce 1,000 units of a product in a month. At the end of the month the production report shows 950 units were made. The controlling function identifies the shortfall and investigates why the plan was not met.
\end{example}

\subsection{Steps of the controlling process}

The controlling process consists of four basic steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \term{Establish standards.} Set quantitative or qualitative targets (e.g., budgeted cost, sales volume).
  \item \term{Measure actual performance.} Collect data on what really happened.
  \item \term{Compare actual with standards.} Compute differences (variances).
  \item \term{Take corrective action.} Adjust resources, processes or goals to bring performance back on track.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A sales department sets a monthly sales target of \$50,000 (standard). In March the actual sales are \$42,000. The variance is \$(8,000). The manager decides to launch a promotion to boost sales in April.
\end{example}

\subsection{Performance measurement tools}

Several tools help managers quantify performance:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \term{Budgeting.} A financial plan that expresses expected revenues and expenses.
  \item \term{Variance analysis.} The calculation of differences between budgeted and actual figures, often expressed as favorable (F) or unfavorable (U).
  \item \term{Balanced scorecard.} A framework that adds non‑financial perspectives (customer, internal processes, learning and growth) to financial measures.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}
A company’s balanced scorecard includes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Financial: Return on investment (ROI);
  \item Customer: Net promoter score;
  \item Internal: Production cycle time;
  \item Learning: Employee training hours.
\end{itemize}
Each perspective has a target and is reviewed quarterly.
\end{example}

\subsection{Simple variance calculation}

For a single cost element, variance is computed as:

\[
\text{Variance} = \text{Actual} - \text{Budgeted}
\]

A positive result indicates an unfavorable variance for costs (spending more than planned); a negative result is favorable.

\begin{example}
Budgeted material cost = \$12,000. Actual material cost = \$13,500.

\[
\text{Variance} = 13{,}500 - 12{,}000 = 1{,}500
\]

The material cost variance is \$1,500 unfavorable, signalling that material usage or price was higher than expected.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Identify a process in your department. Write down the planned target, the actual result, and compute the variance. Decide whether the variance requires corrective action.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define \term{controlling} in your own words and give a non‑financial example.
  \item List the four steps of the controlling process and match each step with an activity from a retail store.
  \item A department budgeted \$8,000 for travel. The actual travel expense was \$6,500. Calculate the variance and state whether it is favorable or unfavorable.
  \item Choose one perspective of the balanced scorecard and suggest a suitable performance indicator for a university library.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Controlling & Monitoring performance, comparing with targets, and correcting deviations. \\
Standard & A planned quantitative or qualitative target used for comparison. \\
Variance & The numerical difference between actual performance and the standard. \\
Budget & A detailed financial plan that sets expected revenues and expenses. \\
Balanced scorecard & A performance‑measurement framework that adds customer, internal, and learning perspectives to financial metrics. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\lessonlabel{9}
\section{Change Management and Innovation}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item define \term{change management} and explain its main steps;
  \item describe the role of \term{innovation} in organisations;
  \item apply a simple change model to a concrete situation;
  \item distinguish between incremental and radical innovation.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Change management}

\term{Change management} is the systematic approach to preparing, supporting and helping individuals, teams and organisations make organisational change. A typical process contains four stages:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Preparation.} Identify the need for change, define objectives and secure leadership support.
  \item \textbf{Planning.} Develop a detailed plan, allocate resources, and design communication strategies.
  \item \textbf{Implementation.} Execute the plan, monitor progress and manage resistance.
  \item \textbf{Evaluation.} Review outcomes, capture lessons learned and embed the change into routine practice.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{example}
A university introduces a new online grading system. In the preparation stage the IT department surveys lecturers to confirm the need. During planning they schedule training sessions and create a help‑desk. Implementation involves rolling out the software to one faculty first, collecting feedback and adjusting. Evaluation compares the time taken to enter grades before and after, and updates the user guide for future semesters.
\end{example}

\subsection{Innovation}

\term{Innovation} is the creation and implementation of new ideas, processes, products or services that add value. Innovation can be classified by scope:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Incremental innovation} – small improvements to existing products or processes (e.g., adding a dark‑mode option to an app).
  \item \textbf{Radical innovation} – breakthrough changes that create new markets or fundamentally alter existing ones (e.g., the introduction of MOOCs).
\end{itemize}

Innovation requires an environment that encourages experimentation, tolerates failure and provides resources for development.

\begin{example}
A campus library adopts RFID tags for books. This is incremental: it speeds up checkout but does not change the core service of lending books. In contrast, launching a fully virtual reality learning platform represents radical innovation, as it reshapes how students interact with course material.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} When faced with a proposed change, ask: (1) Have we identified clear objectives and a plan for people to adopt the change? (2) Does the change introduce a new way of creating value, and is it incremental or radical? If both answers are affirmative, we are dealing with change management that involves innovation.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item List the four stages of change management and give one activity that belongs to each stage for a new student portal.
  \item Classify the following as incremental or radical innovation: (a) adding a chatbot to the admissions website, (b) replacing all face‑to‑face lectures with a fully immersive VR curriculum.
  \item Explain why evaluating a change after implementation is essential for sustaining innovation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Change management & Structured process for planning and guiding organisational change. \\
Innovation & Introduction of new ideas, methods, or products that create value. \\
Incremental innovation & Small, continuous improvements to existing offerings. \\
Radical innovation & Major, disruptive changes that create new markets or behaviours. \\
Evaluation & Systematic review of outcomes to learn and embed improvements. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

